WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.746
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:17.330
Unit twenty-one: Identity and Belonging. In this unit, you will explore how identity is shaped by many overlapping factors.

4
00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:19.829
intersectionality.

5
00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:27.892
intersectionality means the way different identities, such as gender and class, combine to shape experience.

6
00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:32.479
Intersectionality explains why some groups face multiple disadvantages.

7
00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:36.400
Your turn. Say: intersectionality.

8
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.612
intersectionality.

9
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:42.978
hybrid identity.

10
00:00:43.678 --> 00:00:49.392
hybrid identity means an identity combining elements of more than one culture.

11
00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:53.596
Children of migrants often develop a hybrid identity.

12
00:00:54.296 --> 00:00:57.610
Your turn. Say: hybrid identity.

13
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:01.683
hybrid identity.

14
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:04.393
identity politics.

15
00:01:05.093 --> 00:01:09.704
identity politics means politics based on shared group identities.

16
00:01:10.004 --> 00:01:14.372
Identity politics is praised by some and criticised by others.

17
00:01:15.072 --> 00:01:18.967
Your turn. Say: identity politics.

18
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.330
identity politics.

19
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:25.596
sense of place.

20
00:01:26.296 --> 00:01:30.154
sense of place means a strong emotional connection to a location.

21
00:01:30.454 --> 00:01:33.510
Villages give older people a deep sense of place.

22
00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.013
Your turn. Say: sense of place.

23
00:01:39.713 --> 00:01:41.074
sense of place.

24
00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:43.073
otherness.

25
00:01:43.773 --> 00:01:47.920
otherness means the quality of being seen as different or foreign.

26
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.986
Migrants often describe a feeling of otherness.

27
00:01:51.686 --> 00:01:54.559
Your turn. Say: otherness.

28
00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:58.237
otherness.

29
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.623
marginalisation.

30
00:02:01.323 --> 00:02:04.878
marginalisation means pushing a group to the edge of society.

31
00:02:05.178 --> 00:02:08.838
Marginalisation limits access to education and jobs.

32
00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:12.631
Your turn. Say: marginalisation.

33
00:02:15.331 --> 00:02:16.611
marginalisation.

34
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:19.163
self-determination.

35
00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:24.533
self-determination means the right of a people to decide their own future.

36
00:02:24.833 --> 00:02:28.435
Self-determination was central to decolonisation.

37
00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:32.507
Your turn. Say: self-determination.

38
00:02:35.207 --> 00:02:36.580
self-determination.

39
00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:38.631
nationhood.

40
00:02:39.331 --> 00:02:43.362
nationhood means the state of being a nation with a shared identity.

41
00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:46.532
Language and history shape a sense of nationhood.

42
00:02:47.232 --> 00:02:50.500
Your turn. Say: nationhood.

43
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.236
nationhood.

44
00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.459
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:57.759 --> 00:03:00.989
Rithy, what does it mean to be Cambodian today?

46
00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:08.047
Rarely is it a single thing. Language, religion, place and history all overlap.

47
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:13.163
That's intersectionality in action. And for the diaspora?

48
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:20.736
Many develop a hybrid identity. Not only do they speak two languages, but they think in two cultures.

49
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.864
Do they feel a sense of otherness?

50
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.939
Often, in both places. Seldom do they feel fully at home anywhere.

51
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:35.014
Yet nationhood can unite people across differences.

52
00:03:36.317 --> 00:03:42.150
So long as it doesn't lead to the marginalisation of minorities. Only then is it truly inclusive.

53
00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.352
Grammar focus: Inversion and emphasis (extended).

54
00:03:48.652 --> 00:03:51.070
Vary inversion forms for emphasis.

55
00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:58.492
Rarely is it a single thing. Not only do they speak two languages, but they think in two cultures.

56
00:03:58.792 --> 00:04:01.105
Only then is it truly inclusive.

57
00:04:01.405 --> 00:04:06.226
Varying inversion forms adds rhetorical weight without sounding repetitive.

58
00:04:07.217 --> 00:04:09.660
Now read and listen. Who Am I?.

59
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:16.116
Identity was once thought to be simple: a person belonged to one nation, one religion, one people.

60
00:04:17.417 --> 00:04:23.224
Today, scholars emphasise that identities are multiple, fluid and overlapping.

61
00:04:24.517 --> 00:04:31.218
The concept of intersectionality shows how gender, class and ethnicity combine to shape experience.

62
00:04:32.517 --> 00:04:38.417
Migrants and their children often develop hybrid identities, belonging partly to several cultures.

63
00:04:39.717 --> 00:04:44.851
Not only can this bring creativity, but it can also bring a painful sense of otherness.

64
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:51.923
Nationhood can provide unity, yet it may also lead to the marginalisation of minorities.

65
00:04:53.217 --> 00:04:59.489
Self-determination remains a powerful principle for peoples seeking control over their future.

66
00:05:00.783 --> 00:05:05.584
Seldom has the question 'Who am I?' been asked so widely, or answered so diversely.

67
00:05:06.883 --> 00:05:09.652
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:09.952 --> 00:05:13.066
Pushing a group to the edge of society is blank.

69
00:05:13.366 --> 00:05:18.703
A, marginalisation. B, nationhood. C, sense of place.

70
00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:25.750
The answer is A: marginalisation.

71
00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.329
Seldom blank such diversity in one city.

72
00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:35.966
A, we see. B, do we see. C, we do see.

73
00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.333
The answer is B: do we see.

74
00:05:43.817 --> 00:05:47.337
Not only blank talented, but she is also kind.

75
00:05:47.637 --> 00:05:51.511
A, she is. B, is she. C, she.

76
00:05:55.517 --> 00:05:57.865
The answer is B: is she.

77
00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:02.638
An identity combining several cultures is a blank.

78
00:06:02.938 --> 00:06:08.821
A, hybrid identity. B, identity politics. C, otherness.

79
00:06:12.817 --> 00:06:15.954
The answer is A: hybrid identity.

80
00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.713
According to the text, how do scholars now describe identities?

81
00:06:22.013 --> 00:06:28.535
A, simple and fixed. B, multiple, fluid and overlapping. C, purely national.

82
00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:36.291
The answer is B: multiple, fluid and overlapping.

83
00:06:37.783 --> 00:06:43.640
Now it's your turn. Part one: Which aspects of your identity matter most to you?

84
00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:54.806
Part two: Use one inversion structure and explain how two aspects interact.

85
00:07:01.106 --> 00:07:11.037
Rarely do I think of myself in only one way. I'm Khmer, a student and a Buddhist, and not only do these shape my values, but they also influence how others see me.

86
00:07:11.337 --> 00:07:13.151
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:19.774
Great work today! You learned eight new words and inversion and emphasis (extended).

88
00:07:20.074 --> 00:07:24.085
Today's value is self-confidence. Believe in yourself.

89
00:07:24.385 --> 00:07:27.200
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.152
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

